# Normalise ALL text to LF in the index and in the working tree.
#
# Why: this repo is edited on Windows (OneDrive) but every consumer is Linux —
# the Ansible control node, the managed hosts, and every shell/systemd/Jinja file
# it ships. CRLF working copies caused three separate incidents on 2026-08-20:
#   * a 100-line change showed up as a 640-line diff after a file was copied to .20
#   * local_rules.xml appeared to DELETE all 31 Wazuh rules (it was only line endings)
#   * a literal string edit silently failed to match because of a trailing \r
# With eol=lf the working copy is LF too, so files can be copied to Linux hosts
# verbatim - no `sed 's/\r$//'` step, and diffs mean what they say.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly text (belt and braces for the ones that ship to Linux)
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.j2    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.conf  text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.rules text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer   text eol=lf
*.target  text eol=lf

# Binary - never touch these
*.gz    binary
*.tgz   binary
*.zip   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
# Canary share-decoys are random binary blobs that merely LOOK like office docs.
# `file` reports them as "data". Treating them as text corrupts them: a plain
# sed 's/$//' sweep on 2026-08-20 damaged two before git restore recovered them.
*.docx  binary
*.doc   binary
*.xls   binary
*.bak   binary
*.pem   -text
*.crt   -text
*.key   -text
